  "reasoning": "The claim that \"lightning never strikes the same place twice\" is a common myth that is factually false. Lightning can and frequently does strike the exact same location multiple times, particularly tall, isolated, or conductive structures. According to the National Weather Service, prominent structures such as the Empire State Building are struck by lightning an average of 25 times per year. Furthermore, individual lightning flashes themselves often consist of multiple strokes that follow the exact same ionized channel to the ground.",
